oints are those with \\(y\\ge n-k+1\\); there are exactly \\(k(k+1)/2\\) of them.  \n   - Pair these points as \\(\\{(i,n-k+1),(i+1,n-k+2)\\}\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).  \n   - For each pair draw the line through its two points; its slope is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{i}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\). Hence all these \\(k\\) lines are sunny and distinct.  \n   - Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies either on one of the chosen horizontal lines or on one of the sunny lines, so the two required conditions are satisfied.\n\n   This construction works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) **except** the case \\(k=n-1\\) when \\(n\\) is odd, because then the number of uncovered points would be \\(\\frac{(n-1)n}{2}\\), an odd number, while each sunny line contains an even number of points (see the impossibility proof below).\n\n5. **Impossibility when \\(n\\) is odd and \\(k=n-1\\).**  \n   Assume, for contradiction, that a configuration with exactly one non‑sunny line and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists for odd \\(n\\).  \n   *Parity Lemma.*  \n   For any sunny line \\(L\\) the set \\(L\\cap S_n\\) contains an **even** number of points.  \n   *Proof.* A sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families (rows, columns, anti‑diagonals). Hence it meets each row, each column and each anti‑diagonal in at most one point.  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) can be partitioned into the \\(n\\) anti‑diagonals\n   \\[\n   D_t=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t\\},\\qquad t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1,\n   \\]\n   where \\(|D_t|=t-1\\).  \n   Since a sunny line meets each \\(D_t\\) in at most one point, the number of points of \\(L\\cap S_n\\) equals the number of anti‑diagonals it meets.  \n   For a sunny line the equation can be written \\(y=mx+c\\) with \\(m\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\). Solving \\(a+ma+c=t\\) for integer \\(a\\) shows that the set of admissible \\(t\\) is symmetric with respect to the middle anti‑diagonal \\(t=n+2\\); consequently the line meets the anti‑diagonals in pairs, giving an e