ng \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x - i + (m+1) .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\((4)\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny, and because the intercept \\(-i+(m+1)\\) is different for distinct \\(i\\), the lines are distinct.\n\nTake any point \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\).  \nFrom the description (3) we have \\(b-a = m+1-i\\) for a unique integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  \nRearranging gives \\(b = a - i + (m+1)\\), which is precisely the equation of \\(L_i\\).  \nHence \\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  \nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_i\\}_{i=1}^{k}\\) together cover the whole set \\(T_k\\).\n\n#### 1.3  The whole family  \n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{H_1,\\dots ,H_{m}\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).  \nTherefore for every pair \\((n,k)\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the required configuration exists **provided** the construction does not break down.\n\nThe construction fails only when the set \\(T_k\\) cannot be covered by the \\(k\\) lines \\((4)\\).  \nA direct inspection shows that this happens precisely for \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\):\n* here \\(m=n-k=1\\), so \\(T_2=\\{(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}\\);\n* the three points are not collinear with slope \\(1\\), and a line of slope \\(1\\) can contain at most one of them.  \nHence two sunny lines cannot cover \\(T_2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of the pair \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that for \\(n=3\\) there exists a family of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(2,1),\\;(3,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,2),\\;(1,3).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince none of these points lies on the line \\(x+y=0\\), the unique non‑sunny line must be horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or vertical (\\(x=c\\)).\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is horizontal*, say \\(y=1\\), it contains the three points \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\).  \nThe remaining points are \\((1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\).  \nAny sunny line has slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently it cannot be horizontal, vertical, nor have slope \\(-1\\).  \nThe three remaining points are pairwise aligned only horizontally or vertically, so a sunny line can contain **at most one** of them.  \nThus two sunny lines cannot cover the three points – a contradiction.\n\n*If the non‑sunny line is vertical*, the same argument (by symmetry of the coordinates) leads to the same contradiction.\n\nHence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for \\(n=3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Final answer\n\nCombining the construction of Section 1 (which works for every \\((n,k)\\) except \\((3,2)\\)) with the impossibility proved in Section 2, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n=3:\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\};\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\ge 4:\\quad k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThus for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly as described above.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
