al, vertical or diagonal line).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(k\\) sunny and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \nUsing (1.1) and the bound \\(n\\) for a non‑sunny line we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n   \\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-k(n-2).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}=:K_{\\max }(n).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) this gives \\(K_{\\max }(3)=3\\) and \\(K_{\\max }(4)=3\\); for \\(n\\ge5\\) the right‑hand side is a non‑decreasing integer sequence\n\\[\nK_{\\max }(5)=3,\\;K_{\\max }(6)=3,\\;K_{\\max }(7)=4,\\;K_{\\max }(8)=5,\\dots\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more than \\(K_{\\max }(n)\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n#### 3.1  \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}\\).  \nA direct case analysis (using (1.1)) shows:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): \\(y=1, y=2\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=-2x+5\\) (through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n\nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible because after removing any two non‑sunny lines at most three points of \\(S_3\\) remain uncovered, while two sunny lines can cover at most four points and a direct check shows that the three uncovered points cannot be paired by sunny lines.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ for }n=3 } .\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2  \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(S_4\\) contains ten points.  By (2.1) we have \\(k\\le3\\).  \nExplicit families are:\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).  \n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \\(y=1,2,3\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=-2x+7\\quad\\text{(through }(1,4)\\text{ and }(2,2)).\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  The non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n  \\[\n  D:\\;x+y=5\n  \\]\n\n  and the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&:y=x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  L_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n  L_{3}&:y=-2x+5   &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\nA direct verification shows that every point of \\(S_4\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines and that the three sunny lines are distinct and have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible: after removing any two non‑sunny lines the set of uncovered points contains three points that are pairwise non‑collinear with a sunny slope, so two sunny lines cannot cover them.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ for }n=4 } .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  General construction for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge5\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max }(n)\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad t=n-k\\;(>0).\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }-1),\n\\]\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t-1).\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le t-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \n\\(D\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); each \\(V_i\\) contains the points \\((i,1),\\dots ,(i,n+1-i)\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The uncovered set  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is **not** on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) iff  \n\n\\[\na\\ge t\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=t+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k+1\\) and using \\(a+b\\le n\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+1\\).  \nConsequently the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,s)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k+1,\\;1\\le s\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\na \\((k+1)\\times(k+1)\\) square.  Hence \\(|U|=(k+1)^2\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Sunny lines covering \\(U\\)\n\nPair the points of \\(U\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)\\leftrightarrow(2,2),\\\\\n&(2,1)\\leftrightarrow(3,2),\\\\\n&\\dots\\\\\n&(k,1)\\leftrightarrow(k+1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach pair consists of two d