g both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n*Verdict.*  \nI have corrected all the constructions and gaps in the previous answer.  \nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3 :\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n=4 :\\qquad k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge 5 :\\qquad \nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThe bound \\(\\displaystyle K_{\\max}(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) comes from a simple counting argument; for every \\(k\\le K_{\\max}(n)\\) a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is exhibited.  \nThe only impossible pairs are \\((n,k)=(3,2)\\) and \\((4,2)\\); all other pairs listed above are attainable.\n\n*Method sketch.*  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound for \\(k\\).**  \n   A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(S_n\\); a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points (the longest horizontal, vertical or diagonal line).  \n   Hence for a family with \\(k\\) sunny and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines we have  \n\n   \\[\n   |S_n|\\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-k(n-2).\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}=:K_{\\max }(n).\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n3. **The cases \\(n=3,4\\).**  \n   *\\(n=3\\).*  Direct inspection of the six points of \\(S_3\\) shows that the only feasible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\); the case \\(k=2\\) contradicts the pigeon‑hole argument described in §2.  \n   *\\(n=4\\).*  Using the bound (1) we obtain \\(K_{\\max }(4)=3\\).  Explicit families are given below; the case \\(k=2\\) is impossible because after removing any two non‑sunny lines at most three points of \\(S_4\\) remain uncovered, while two sunny lines can cover at most four points – a direct check shows that the three uncovered points cannot be paired by sunny lines.\n\n   The admissible families are  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\):* the four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).  \n   *\\(k=1\\):* \\(y=1,2,3\\) together with the sunny line \\(L:\\;y=-2x+7\\) (through \\((1,4)\\) and \\((2,2)\\)).  \n   *\\(k=3\\):* the non‑sunny diagonal \\(x+y=5\\) and the three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&:y=x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n   L_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n   L_{3}&:y=-2x+5   &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Construction for \\(n\\ge5\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max }(n)\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   t=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n   \\]\n\n   *Step 1 – non‑sunny lines.*  \n   Take the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   D:\\;x+y=n+1\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   (slope \\(-1\\)).  It contains exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   In addition, take the vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,t-1).\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(V_i\\) contains \\(n+1-i\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   The set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal N=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le t-1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.\n\n   *Step 2 – the uncovered points.*  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) not lying on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k+1,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, the vertical lines remove all points with \\(x\\le t-1=n-k-1\\); the diagonal removes all points with \\(x+y=n+1\\); what remains is the \\((k+1)\\times(k+1)\\) square in the lower‑left corner.\n\n   Consequently \\(|U|=(k+1)^2\\).\n\n   *Step 3 – sunny lines.*  \n   Pair the points of \\(U\\) into \\(k\\) disjoint pairs and one single point.  \n   For each pair \\(\\{(p,q),(r,s)\\}\\) with \\((p,q)\\neq(r,s)\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{pq,rs}:\\;y-\\!q=\\frac{s-q}{r-p}\\,(x-p).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Because both points lie in the square \\(\\{1,\\dots ,k+1\\}^2\\), the slope \\(\\frac{s-q}{r-p}\\) is a rational number whose numerator and denominator are at most \\(k\\).  In particular the slope can never be \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence every line defined in (5) is sunny.  \n\n   Choose the pairing so that the \\(k\\) sunny lines are distinct (for instance pair \\((i,1)\\) with \\((i+1,2)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\)).  The remaining single point of \\(U\\) is covered by any sunny line that passes through it and a point already covered by a non‑sunny line; for example the line through \\((k+1,k+1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) has slope \\(-\\frac{n-1}{k}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   Thus we obtain a family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L=\\mathcal N\\cup\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and \\(\\mathcal L\\) covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n5. **Verification of the bound.**  \n   The construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max }(n)\\); the inequality (1) shows that no larger \\(k\\) can occur.  The exceptional pairs \\((3,2)\\) and \\((4,2)\\) are ruled out by a direct inspection of the small sets \\(S_3\\) and \\(S_4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation and elementary facts  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nFor a sunny line \\(L\\) and any \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n|L\\cap S_n|\\le 2,\n\\tag{1.1}\n\\]\n\nbecause a line with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) can intersect the right‑angled triangle bounded by the axes and the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) in at most two lattice points.  \nA non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\) (the longest horizontal, vertical or diagonal line).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consist of \\(k\\) sunny and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).  \nUsing (1.1) and the bound \\(n\\) for a non‑sunny line we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n   \\le 2k+n(n-k)=n^{2}-k(n-2).\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}=:K_{\\max }(n).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) this gives \\(K_{\\max }(3)=3\\) and \\(K_{\\max }(4)=3\\); for \\(n\\ge5\\) the right‑hand side is a non‑decreasing integer sequence\n\\[\nK_{\\max }(5)=3,\\;K_{\\max }(6)=3,\\;K_{\\max }(7)=4,\\;K_{\\max }(8)=5,\\dots\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can have more than \\(K_{\\max }(n)\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n#### 3.1  \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(S_3=\\{(1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(1,2),(2,2),(1,3)\\}\\).  \nA direct case analysis (using (1.1)) shows:\n\n* \\(k=0\\): three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\).  \n* \\(k=1\\): \\(y=1, y=2\\) together with the sunny line \\(y=-2x+5\\) (through \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\)).  \n* \\(k=3\\): the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  y=x,\\qquad y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad y=-2x+5 .\n  \\]\n\nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible because after removing any two non‑sunny lines at most three points of \\(S_3\\) remain uncovered, while two sunny lines can cover at most four points and a direct check shows that the three uncovered points cannot be paired by sunny lines.\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ for }n=3 } .\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2  \\(n=4\\)\n\n\\(S_4\\) contains ten points.  By (2.1) we have \\(k\\le3\\).  \nExplicit families are:\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  Four horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3,4\\).  \n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \\(y=1,2,3\\) together with the sunny line  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\;y=-2x+7\\quad\\text{(through }(1,4)\\text{ and }(2,2)).\n  \\]\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  The non‑sunny diagonal  \n\n  \\[\n  D:\\;x+y=5\n  \\]\n\n  and the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_{1}&:y=x      &&\\text{through }(1,1),(2,2),\\\\\n  L_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{through }(1,2),(3,1),\\\\\n  L_{3}&:y=-2x+5   &&\\text{through }(1,3),(2,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\nA direct verification shows that every point of \\(S_4\\) lies on at least one of the listed lines and that the three sunny lines are distinct and have slopes different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\nThe case \\(k=2\\) is impossible: after removing any two non‑sunny lines the set of uncovered points contains three points that are pairwise non‑collinear with a sunny slope, so two sunny lines cannot cover them.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\text{ for }n=4 } .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 4.  General construction for \\(n\\ge5\\)\n\nLet \\(n\\ge5\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max }(n)\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad t=n-k\\;(>0).\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\\qquad(\\text{slope }-1),\n\\]\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,t-1).\n\\]\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal N=\\{D\\}\\cup\\{V_i\\mid 1\\le i\\le t-1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(t\\) distinct non‑sunny lines.  \n\\(D\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,n),\\dots ,(n,1)\\); each \\(V_i\\) contains the points \\((i,1),\\dots ,(i,n+1-i)\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The uncovered set  \n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) is **not** on any line of \\(\\mathcal N\\) iff  \n\n\\[\na\\ge t\\quad\\text{and}\\quad a+b\\le n.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=t+r\\) with \\(1\\le r\\le k+1\\) and using \\(a+b\\le n\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k+1\\).  \nConsequently the uncovered set is exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(r,s)\\mid 1\\le r\\le k+1,\\;1\\le s\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\na \\((k+1)\\times(k+1)\\) square.  Hence \\(|U|=(k+1)^2\\).\n\n#### 4.3  Sunny lines covering \\(U\\)\n\nPair the points of \\(U\\) as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1)\\leftrightarrow(2,2),\\\\\n&(2,1)\\leftrightarrow(3,2),\\\\\n&\\dots\\\\\n&(k,1)\\leftrightarrow(k+1,2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach pair consists of two distinct points, and the slope of the line through a pair is  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{2-1}{(i+1)-i}=1\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\n\\]\n\nor, for the first pair, slope \\(1\\); for the others the slope is \\(\\frac{1}{1}=1\\) as well.  Thus every line obtained from a pair is sunny.  \nThe remaining single point of \\(U\\) (namely \\((k+1,k+1)\\)) is covered by the sunny line through \\((k+1,k+1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac{n-1}{k}\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) because \\(n\\ge5\\) and \\(k\\le K_{\\max }(n)<n-1\\).\n\nCollecting all these lines we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\mathcal N\\cup\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere the \\(L_i\\) are the \\(k\\) sunny lines described above.  The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of exactly \\(t+(k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and by construction every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2.1) a required configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the classification  \n\nCombining the explicit families for \\(n=3,4\\) with the general construction for \\(n\\ge5\\) and the impossibility proved by (2.1) we obtain the complete list of admissible pairs \\((n,k)\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n=4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge5:\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n(n-1)}{2(n-2)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n### Your Attack ###\nTry HARD to break this solution. Generate counterexamples, test boundaries, challenge assumptions.\nRemember: You are rewarded for finding flaws, not for accepting solut