frac{n-1}{k}\\neq0,\n\\infty,-1\\) because \\(k\\le K_{\\max }'(n)<n-1\\).  Consequently we obtain exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Case \\(k\\ge4\\).*  \nWhen \\(k\\ge4\\) the uncovered set (3.5) is too large for the pairing method.\nWe replace some of the vertical lines in \\(\\mathcal N\\) by horizontal lines\n\\(H_j:\\;y=j\\) (with \\(1\\le j\\le t-1\\)) so that the total number of uncovered\npoints becomes precisely \\(2k\\).  A short calculation shows that this is\npossible exactly when \\(k\\le K_{\\max }'(n)\\) (the inequality (2.2) is equivalent\nto the existence of a choice of \\((t-1)\\) lines among the \\(2(t-1)\\) possible\nvertical/horizontal lines whose total coverage equals \\(tn-(t-1)-2k\\)).  With the\nnew non‑sunny family the uncovered set consists of \\(2k\\) points; they are\npaired arbitrarily and each pair yields a sunny line as before.  Hence we obtain\na family of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2.2) a required configuration\nexists.\n\n### 4.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* \\(n=3\\):  Direct inspection of the six points of \\(S_3\\) shows that the only\n  feasible values are \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n* \\(n=4\\):  A similar exhaustive check yields \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n* \\(n\\ge5\\):  The bound (2.2) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  K_{\\max }'(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n  \\]\n\n  The construction of §3 produces configurations for all\n  \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max }'(n)\\}\\).  Hence these are exactly the admissible\n  values.\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n=4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge5:\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
