heck gives \\(k=0,1,3\\).  \n   *\\(n\\ge5\\).*  Formula (2.2) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   K_{\\max }(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2\\,(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   The construction of §3 produces configurations for all\n   \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max }(n)\\}\\); therefore these are exactly the\n   admissible values.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Notation  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(\\ell\\) be a sunny line.  Then \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of\n\\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  The triangle \\(\\{a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is bounded by the three\nlines \\(x=0\\), \\(y=0\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\).  Because the slope of \\(\\ell\\) is\ndifferent from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), \\(\\ell\\) can intersect each of the three\nboundary lines in at most one lattice point.  Hence \\(\\ell\\) can meet the\nboundary in at most three points, and at most two of those can lie inside the\ntriangle.  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\). ∎  \n\n#### Lemma 2  \nA non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most\n\\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Such a line is parallel to one side of the triangle; the maximal\nintersection with the triangle is a segment of length \\(n\\) measured in lattice\nsteps, giving at most \\(n\\) lattice points. ∎  \n\nLet a configuration consist of \\(k\\) sunny and \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nBy Lemma 2 the non‑sunny part can cover at most \\(tn\\) points.\nBy Lemma 1 the sunny part can cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nAny two distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at least one lattice point\n(because the three families of non‑sunny lines are pairwise transversal).\nThe smallest possible total number of such intersections is \\(t-1\\); th