n cover at most \\(tn\\) points.\nBy Lemma 1 the sunny part can cover at most \\(2k\\) points.\n\nAny two distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in at least one lattice point\n(because the three families of non‑sunny lines are pairwise transversal).\nThe smallest possible total number of such intersections is \\(t-1\\); this is\nrealised by arranging the \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines so that their intersection graph\nis a tree.  Hence the maximal number of *distinct* points covered by the\nnon‑sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\ntn-(t-1).\n\\]\n\nAdding the contribution of the sunny lines we obtain the inequality  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le tn-(t-1)+2k\n               =n^2-k(n-3).\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\) yields  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2\\,(n-3)}=:K_{\\max }(n).\n\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nThus (2.1) is a necessary condition for the existence of a required family\nof lines.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le K_{\\max }(n)\\) and set \\(t=n-k\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny lines  \n\nChoose non‑negative integers \\(a,b\\) with \\(a+b=t\\).  \nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,a)\n\\]\n\nand the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad(j=1,\\dots ,b).\n\\]\n\nThese \\(t\\) lines are non‑sunny and are pairwise distinct.\n\nA point \\((x,y)\\in S_n\\) fails to lie on any of them precisely when  \n\n\\[\nx>a,\\qquad y>b,\\qquad x+y\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThe set described by (3.1) is a right‑angled lattice triangle whose legs have\nlength \\(k=n-a-b\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|U|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering the triangle  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(0\\le c\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x-(a-b)-c .\n\\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(L_c\\) have slope \\(1\\); therefore they are sunny.\nInside the triangle \\(U\\) the points with a fixed value of \\(x-y\\) lie on a\nsingle line \\(L_c\\), and for a given \\(c\\) the line \\(L_c\\) contains exactly\n\\(k-c\\) points of \\(U\\).  Consequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S=\\{L_c\\mid 0\\le c\\le k-1\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines and covers every point of \\(U\\).\n\nNo line of \\(\\mathcal S\\) coincides with any of the chosen vertical or\nhorizontal lines, because their slopes differ.  Hence the total family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_i\\mid1\\le i\\le a\\}\\cup\\{H_j\\mid1\\le j\\le b\\}\\cup\\mathcal S\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(a+b+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2.1) a required configuration exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Small values of \\(n\\)\n\n* \\(n=3\\).  The set \\(S_3\\) consists of six points.  Exhaustive checking shows\nthat the only possible numbers of sunny lines are \\(0,1,3\\).\n\n* \\(n=4\\).  A similar exhaustive search yields the admissible values\n\\(0,1,3\\).\n\n* \\(n\\ge5\\).  Formula (2.1) gives  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\max }(n)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2\\,(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe construction of §3 produces configurations for all\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,K_{\\max }(n)\\}\\); therefore these are exactly the\nadmissible values.\n\n---\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&n=3:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n=4:\\;k\\in\\{0,1,3\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&n\\ge5:\\;k\\in\\Bigl\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}{2\\,(n-3)}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
