 \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly\n\\(k\\) sunny lines for each of the three remaining possibilities \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n---\n\n**(i)  \\(k=0\\).**  \nTake the following \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\);\n* horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,\\dots ,\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\).\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) or\n\\(b\\le\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\); consequently it lies on at least one of the chosen\nlines.  All lines are non‑sunny, so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n---\n\n**(ii)  \\(k=1\\).**  \nProceed as in (i) but omit one of the lines, say the vertical line \\(x=1\\).\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) that are not covered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(U\\) is a right‑angled lattice triangle of side length \\(1\\); it\ncontains the two points \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\).  \nThe line of slope \\(1\\) passing through these two points,\n\\(y=x-1\\), is sunny and contains both points of \\(U\\).  Adding this single\nsunny line to the remaining \\(n-1\\) non‑sunny lines yields a configuration\nwith exactly one sunny line.\n\n---\n\n**(iii)  \\(k=3\\).**  \nLet \\(t=n-3\\).  Choose \\(a\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,a\\) and\n\\(b\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,b\\) with \\(a+b=t\\) (any split works).\nThese \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines cover all points of \\(S_n\\) except those\nsatisfying  \n\n\\[\nx>a,\\qquad y>b,\\qquad x+y\\le n+1 .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\nThe set described by (5.1) is a right‑angled lattice triangle of side length\n\\(3\\); it contains exactly six points.  They can be paired as  \n\n\\[\n\\{(a+1,b+2),(a+2,b+1)\\},\\;\n\\{(a+1,b+3),(a+3,b+1)\\},\\;\n\\{(a+2,b+3),(a+3,b+2)\\},\n\\]\n\nand each pair lies on a line of slope \\(1\\), which is sunny.  \nThus the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\ny=x-(a-b),\\quad y=x-(a-b)-1,\\quad y=x-(a-b)-2\n\\]\n\ntogether cover the six uncovered points.  Adding them to the \\(t\\) non‑sunny\nlines gives a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly three sunny\nlines.\n\nHence configurations exist for \\(k=0,1,3\\).\n\n#### 6.  Final answer  \n\nFrom Sections 3 and 4 we have proved that no configuration can contain\n\\(k\\ge4\\) sunny lines and that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  \nSection 5 shows that the three remaining values \\(k=0,1,3\\) are always\nrealizable (for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every }n\\ge3\\text{ the admissible values of }k\\text{ are }k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.}\n\\]"
