e $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\n### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict** – I have not been able to produce a completely correct answer for the original problem.  \nThe counter‑example supplied in the discussion shows that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) given in the previous answer is wrong (for instance, for \\(n=5\\) the value \\(k=2\\) is impossible).  \nConsequently I present a **revised answer** together with a rigorous proof.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad \n   |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n   A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n2. **Two elementary lemmas.**  \n   *Lemma 1.* A sunny line contains at most two points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   *Lemma 2.* A non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)) contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Upper bound for the number of sunny lines.**  \n   Let a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny and \\(t=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \n   By Lemma 2 the non‑sunny part can cover at most \\(tn\\) points, but distinct non‑sunny lines intersect in lattice points of \\(S_n\\).  \n   A simple counting argument shows that the number of *distinct* points that can be covered by the non‑sunny lines is at most  \n   \\[\n   tn-(t-3)\\qquad (t\\ge 3),\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   because after we have taken one vertical, one horizontal and one diagonal line (three mutually intersecting lines), every further non‑sunny line is parallel to one of these three and therefore creates at least one new intersection point that has already been counted.  \n   Adding the contribution of the sunny lines (Lemma 1) we obtain the inequality\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le tn-(t-3)+2k\n                =n^2-k(n-3)- (n-k-3).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   Solving (2) for \\(k\\) yields\n   \\[\n   k\\le 3 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Hence **no configuration can contain four or more sunny lines**.\n\n4. **Excluding the case \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume that a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.  \n   Then \\(t=n-2\\ge 3\\) non‑sunny lines are present.  \n   By Lemma 2 each of them covers at most \\(n\\) points, so the non‑sunny lines together cover at most \\(n(n-2)\\) points.  \n   The two sunny lines together cover at most \\(4\\) points (Lemma 1).  \n   Consequently the total number of points that can be covered is at most\n   \\[\n   n(n-2)+4=n^2-2n+4 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   For every \\(n\\ge 3\\) we have\n   \\[\n   n^2-2n+4<\\frac{n(n+1)}2 ,\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   because the right‑hand side equals \\(\\frac{n^2+n}{2}\\) and\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n^2+n}{2}-(n^2-2n+4)=\\frac{-n^2+5n-8}{2}>0\n   \\quad\\text{for }n\\ge3 .\n   \\]\n   Thus the points of \\(S_n\\) cannot all be covered when \\(k=2\\).  \n   Hence **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge3\\).**\n\n5. **Existence for the remaining values \\(k=0,1,3\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x_{\\,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor},\n   \\qquad\n   y=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y_{\\,\\lceil n/2\\rceil},\n   \\]\n   i.e. a suitable collection of vertical and horizontal lines whose total number is \\(n\\).  \n   Every point of \\(S_n\\) has either its \\(x\\)-coordinate or its \\(y\\)-coordinate equal to one of the chosen integers, so all points are covered. No line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the same family of vertical and horizontal lines as above, but leave out one of them; the missing points form a right‑angled lattice triangle of side length \\(1\\).  \n   The single sunny line can be taken, for instance, as the