er exactly  \n\n    \\[\n    \\sum_{i=1}^{t}(n+1-i)=\\frac{t(2n+2-t)}2\n    \\]\n\n    points of \\(S_n\\).  The points that remain uncovered form the right‑angled\n    lattice triangle  \n\n    \\[\n    T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n    \\]\n\n    which is similar to \\(S_k\\) and contains  \n\n    \\[\n    |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n    \\tag{3.1}\n    \\]\n\n    points.\n\n4.  By Lemma 1 each of the \\(k\\) sunny lines can contain at most\n    \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(T_k\\).  Hence\n\n    \\[\n    k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge |T_k|\n    =\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n    \\tag{3.2}\n    \\]\n\n    The inequality (3.2) holds only for \\(k=0,1,3\\):\n    * if \\(k\\) is even, the left–hand side equals \\(\\frac{k^2}2\\) while the right–hand side\n      equals \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2>\\frac{k^2}2\\);\n    * if \\(k\\) is odd and \\(k\\ge5\\), the left–hand side is\n      \\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2- \\frac{k-1}2<\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\).\n\n    Therefore a covering with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines is impossible for\n    \\(k\\ge4\\) and for \\(k=2\\).\n\n5.  **Existence constructions.**  \n\n    *\\(k=0\\).*  \n    Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny and every\n    \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) satisfies \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), hence lies on one of them.\n\n    *\\(k=1\\).*  \n    Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only point not yet\n    covered is \\((n,1)\\).  Add the sunny line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\);\n    its slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{\\,n-1\\,}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n    *\\(k=3\\).*  \n    Take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\).  The uncovered set is\n    the triangle \\(T_3\\) consisting of the six points  \n\n    \\[\n    (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n    \\]\n\n    They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    L_1 &: y=x-(n-3)      &&\\t