which indeed cover the six points of\n   \\(S_3\\).\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The impossibility proved in step 4 together with the constructions of step 5\n   yields the complete description of the admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nThus a sunny line has a direction vector \\((p,q)\\) with integers satisfying  \n\n\\[\np\\neq0,\\qquad q\\neq0,\\qquad p\\neq -q .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n*If a line is sunny, then it contains at most \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).*\n\n**Proof.**  \nWrite the line in primitive integer direction \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\); then all lattice points on the line are of the form  \n\n\\[\n(x_0,y_0)+t(p,q),\\qquad t\\in\\mathbb Z .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nCondition (1) implies \\(|p|+|q|\\ge3\\).  \nIndeed, if \\(|p|=1\\) then \\(|q|\\ge2\\) because \\(q\\neq0\\) and \\(q\\neq -p\\); similarly\nif \\(|q|=1\\) then \\(|p|\\ge2\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|p|\\ge1,\\qquad |q|\\ge1,\\qquad |p|+|q|\\ge3 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe convex hull of \\(S_n\\) is the right‑angled triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).  A sunny line meets this triangle in a (possibly\ndegenerate) segment whose projection on the \\(x\\)-axis has length at most\n\\(n-1\\).  From (2) the distance in the \\(x\\)-direction between two consecutive\nlattice points on the line equals \\(|p|\\).  Therefore the number of lattice\npoints of the line inside the triangle is at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|p|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(|p|\\ge2\\) then (4) \\(\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \nIf \\(|p|=1\\) then by (3) we have \\(|q|\\ge2\\); the projection on the \\(y\\)-axis\nhas length at most \\(n-1\\) as well, so the number of points is bounded by  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{|q|}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+1\n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThus in every case a sunny line contains at most\n\\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Reduction to a smaller triangle\n\nLet the number of sunny lines be \\(k\\) and the number of non‑sunny lines be\n\\(t=n-k\\).  To maximise the number of points covered by the non‑sunny lines we\nchoose them to be the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=t .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(x=i\\) contains exactly the points \\((i,b)\\) with\n\\(1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\); hence the total number of points covered by the family\n(5) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i=1}^{t}(n+1-i)=\\frac{t(2n+2-t)}2 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set of points of \\(S_n\\) not covered by (5) is  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a\\ge t+1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a'=a-(t)\\) we see that \\((a',b)\\) satisfies\n\\(a'+b\\le k+1\\); thus \\(T_k\\) is a translate of the set \\(S_k\\) and\n\n\\[\n|T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Counting with the sunny lines\n\nApply Lemma 1 to the set \\(T_k\\) (replace \\(n\\) by \\(k\\)).  Each sunny line can\ncontain at most  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\npoints of \\(T_k\\).  Since there are exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, a covering of\n\\(S_n\\) with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines would require  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\ge |T_k|\n=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWe examine (8).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is even*, write \\(k=2m\\).  Then the left–hand side equals\n\\(2m\\cdot m=m\\cdot2m/2=\\frac{k^{2}}2\\), whereas the right–hand side is\n\\(\\frac{k(k+1)}2>\\frac{k^{2}}2\\).  Hence (8) fails for every even \\(k\\ge2\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\) is odd*, write \\(k=2m+1\\).  Then\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{k+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n=(2m+1)(m+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2-\\frac{k-1}{2}.\n\\]\nThus (8) holds only when \\(\\frac{k-1}{2}=0\\), i.e. \\(k=1\\), or when the\nsubtracted term vanishes, which happens for \\(k=3\\) (because then\n\\(\\frac{k-1}{2}=1\\) and the right–hand side equals \\(\\frac{3\\cdot4}{2}=6\\) while the\nleft–hand side equals \\(3\\cdot2=6\\)).  For every odd \\(k\\ge5\\) the left–hand side\nis strictly smaller than the right–hand side, so (8) fails.\n\nCollecting the possibilities we obtain\n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Constructions for the admissible values\n\nWe now exhibit, for every \\(n\\ge3\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing\nexactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for each \\(k\\) in (9).\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\) are all non‑sunny and clearly cover\n  every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  The only point of\n  \\(S_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  Add the line through \\((n,1)\\) and\n  \\((1,2)\\); its slope is \\(-\\dfrac1{n-1}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\), so it is sunny.\n  The resulting family has exactly one sunny line.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) (the set is empty when\n  \\(n=3\\)).  The six points that remain uncovered are  \n\n  \\[\n  (n-2,1),\\;(n-2,2),\\;(n-2,3),\\;(n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n  \\]\n\n  They are covered by the three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y = x-(n-3)               &&\\text{through }(n-2,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{n+2}2 &&\\text{through }(n-2,2),(n,1),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y = -2x+(2n-1)            &&\\text{through }(n-2,3),(n-1,1).\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  Their slopes are \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); the\n  three lines are distinct for every \\(n\\ge3\\).  For \\(n=3\\) the vertical family\n  disappears and the three lines become \\(y=x,\\;y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\;\n  y=-2x+5\\), which indeed cover the six points of \\(S_3\\).\n\nThus for each admissible \\(k\\) we have exhibited a concrete configuration of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final conclusion\n\nThe inequality (8) shows that no configuration with \\(k\\notin\\{0,1,3\\}\\) can\nexist, while the constructions above prove that each of the three numbers\n\\(0,1,3\\) is attainable for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.}\n\\]"
