ext{through }(n-2,1),(n-1,2),\\\\\n    L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{n+2}2 &&\\text{through }(n-2,2),(n,1),\\\\\n    L_3 &: y=-2x+2n-3    &&\\text{through }(n-2,3),(n-1,1),\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n\n    whose slopes are \\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\), none of which is \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n    Together with the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines we obtain \\(n\\) distinct lines,\n    exactly three of them sunny.\n\n    The same three lines work for \\(n=3\\) (the vertical family is empty), so the\n    construction is valid for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n6.  Combining the impossibility proved in step 4 with the explicit constructions\n    of step 5 yields the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For every integer }n\\ge3\\text{ the possible numbers of sunny lines are }k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
