sunny*.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The extreme values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\n*   \\(k=0\\): take the \\(n\\) horizontal lines \\(\\{H_{b}\\}_{b=1}^{n}\\).  \n    Every \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on the line \\(H_{b}\\), so the covering\n    condition holds and no line is sunny.\n\n*   \\(k=n\\): take the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(\\mathcal S\\) of (3).  \n    For a point \\((a,b)\\) we have \\(b-a\\in\\{-(n-1),-(n-3),\\dots ,n-1\\}\\);\n    consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the line \\(S_{b-a}\\).  All \\(n\\) lines are\n    sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=1\\)\n\nChoose the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_1 : y = x \\qquad(\\text{slope }1)\n\\]\n\nand the \\(n-1\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_{2},H_{3},\\dots ,H_{n}.\n\\]\n\nThe point \\((1,1)\\) (the only point of \\(S_n\\) with \\(b=1\\) and \\(a=b\\))\nlies on \\(L_1\\); every other point of \\(S_n\\) has \\(b\\ge 2\\) and therefore\nlies on the horizontal line \\(H_{b}\\).  Hence the covering condition is met\nand exactly one line (namely \\(L_1\\)) is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The intermediate values \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\).  \nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;C_k=\\{c_i=n-2i+1\\mid i=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\;}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(the \\(k\\) largest admissible differences) and  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;S_k=\\{s_j=j+1\\mid j=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\;}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n(the \\(n-k\\) smallest admissible sums).  \n\nConsider the following set of \\(n\\) lines:\n\n* **Sunny lines**  \n  \\[\n  L_i:\\;y=x+c_i\\qquad (c_i\\in C_k,\\;i=1,\\dots ,k).\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n\n  Each \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore all of them are sunny.\n\n* **Non‑sunny diagonal lines**  \n  \\[\n  D_j:\\;x+y=s_j\\qquad (s_j\\in S_k,\\;j=1,\\dots ,n-k).\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  Every \\(D_j\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and hence is\n  non‑sunny.\n\nAll the lines are distinct because the numbers in \\(C_k\\) are distinct and\nthe numbers in \\(S_k\\) are distinct.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Proof that the family covers \\(S_n\\)\n\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta=b-a,\\qquad \\Sigma=a+b .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(\\Sigma\\le n+1\\), the possible values are  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta\\in\\{-(n-1),-(n-3),\\dots ,n-1\\},\\qquad \n\\Sigma\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe distinguish two cases.\n\n* **Case 1.** \\(\\Sigma\\le n-k+1\\).  \n  Then \\(\\Sigma\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-k+1\\}=S_k\\); consequently the point lies on\n  the diagonal line \\(D_{\\Sigma}\\).\n\n* **Case 2.** \\(\\Sigma\\ge n-k+2\\).  \n  Since \\(\\Sigma\\le n+1\\), we have  \n\n  \\[\n  \\Delta = b-a = (\\Sigma-a)-a = \\Sigma-2a \\ge \\Sigma-2\\ge (n-k+2)-2 = n-2k+2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Because \\(\\Delta\\) has the same parity as \\(\\Sigma\\) (both are even or both\n  odd), the smallest admissible value of \\(\\Delta\\) that is \\(\\ge n-2k+2\\) is\n  exactly \\(n-2k+1\\).  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  \\Delta\\in\\{n-2k+1,\\,n-2k+3,\\dots ,n-1\\}=C_k,\n  \\]\n\n  and the point lies on the sunny line \\(L_i\\) with \\(c_i=\\Delta\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines.\nThe construction uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(L_i\\)’s) and\n\\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (the \\(D_j\\)’s).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* covers every point of \\(S_n\\), and  \n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nNo other value can occur because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the\ntotal number of lines, which is \\(n\\). ∎"
