nd \\((2,1)\\). Thus \\(k=3\\) is impossible as well.\n\n### ANSWER_IMPLICATIONS ###\nThe solution’s statement  \n> “For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) all non‑negative integers \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) can occur”  \n\nis false. Already for the smallest admissible \\(n=3\\) the values \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=3\\) cannot be realized. Consequently the correct set of admissible \\(k\\) is **strictly smaller** than \\(\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\); for \\(n=3\\) it is \\(\\{0,1\\}\\). (The exact description for general \\(n\\) remains open, but the “all‑\\(k\\)” claim is disproved.)\n\n### BOUNDARY_CASES ###\n- **BOUNDARY_1:** \\(n=3,\\;k=0\\) – works (three horizontal lines \\(y=1,2,3\\)).  \n- **BOUNDARY_2:** \\(n=3,\\;k=1\\) – works (sunny line \\(y=x\\) plus horizontals \\(y=2,3\\)).  \n- **BOUNDARY_3:** \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\) – **fails** (see Counterexample 1).  \n- **BOUNDARY_4:** \\(n=3,\\;k=3\\) – **fails** (see Counterexample 2).  \n\nThese boundary checks already invalidate the universal claim.\n\n### ASSUMPTION_CHALLENGES ###\n- **CHALLENGE_1:** The proof assumes the difference \\(\\Delta=b-a\\) is always non‑negative when handling “Case 2”. Points with \\(b<a\\) (e.g., \\((3,1)\\) for \\(n=3\\)) are ignored, leaving them uncovered.\n- **CHALLENGE_2:** Parity of \\(\\Delta\\) and the sum \\(\\Sigma=a+b\\) is glossed over. The set \\(C_k=\\{n-2i+1\\}\\) contains numbers of a single parity, yet \\(\\Delta\\) may have the opposite parity, so many points are not matched.\n- **CHALLENGE_3:** The argument that “the smallest admissible value of \\(\\Delta\\) ≥ \\(n-2k+2\\) is \\(n-2k+1\\)” is incorrect when the parity of \\(n-2k+1\\) does not match that of \\(\\Delta\\).\n\n### CRITICAL_FLAWS ###\n- **FLAW_1:** **Neglect of negative differences** – the construction only covers points with \\(b\\ge a\\). Points with \\(b<a\\) are omitted, breaking the covering condition.\n- **FLAW_2:** **Parity mismatch** – the set of allowed differences \\(C_k\\) does not account for both parities, s