oned into \\(k\\)\n   subsets, each subset containing exactly one point of every diagonal in\n   (2).  Such a partition is possible precisely for  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad k\\ge n-2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof of (3).*  \n   – For \\(k=0,1\\) the constructions of Sections 3 and 4 give the required\n   partitions.  \n   – Assume \\(2\\le k\\le n-3\\).  Then the set (2) contains at least the two\n   smallest diagonals \\(D_{2}\\) (size 1) and \\(D_{3}\\) (size 2), i.e. three\n   points.  Because any two points of \\(D_{3}\\) lie on the diagonal\n   \\(D_{3}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)), Lemma 1 forces them to belong to **different**\n   sunny lines.  Consequently at least two sunny lines are already used for\n   the two points of \\(D_{3}\\); the remaining point of \\(D_{2}\\) cannot lie\n   on either of those two lines (any line through \\((1,1)\\) and a point of\n   \\(D_{3}\\) is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), all non‑sunny).  A\n   third sunny line would be needed, contradicting \\(k\\le n-3\\).  Hence no\n   covering exists for \\(2\\le k\\le n-3\\).  \n   – For \\(k=n-2,n-1,n\\) we exhibit explicit constructions (see below).\n\n7. **Explicit constructions for the large values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=n-2\\).*  \n   Use the two largest diagonals \\(D_{n+1},D_{n}\\) as the non‑sunny lines.\n   The remaining \\(n-2\\) diagonals are exactly those listed in (2) with\n   \\(k=n-2\\).  For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-2\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{i}:\\; y = -i\\,x + (i+1).\n   \\]\n\n   One checks that \\(L_{i}\\) meets diagonal \\(D_{j}\\;(2\\le j\\le n-1)\\) in the\n   unique integer point \\((\\,j-i-1,\\;i+1\\,)\\); consequently the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{D_{n+1},D_{n}\\}\\cup\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{n-2}\\}\n   \\]\n\n   consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(n-2\\) of which are sunny,\n   and it covers every point of \\(S_{n}\\).\n\n   *\\(k=n-1\\).*  \n   Take the single largest diagonal \\(D_{n+1}\\) as th