"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have found a complete solution.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k=0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. **every** non\u2011negative integer not exceeding \\(n\\) can occur.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Reduction.**  \n   Use \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny (vertical) lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\).  \n   These lines already cover all lattice points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le n-k\\).  \n   The points that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   After the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a',b')=(a-(n-k),b)\\) the set\n   \\(T_{k}\\) becomes the \u201ctriangular\u201d set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the problem is reduced to the following claim.\n\n2. **Key Lemma (Sunny\u2011Triangle Lemma).**  \n   *For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines that\n   together contain all points of \\(\\Delta_{m}\\).*  \n\n   The lemma is proved by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n   *Base \\(m=1\\).*  \n   The single point \\((1,1)\\) is covered by the line through \\((1,1)\\) and\n   \\((2,2)\\); its slope is \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume the lemma true for \\(m-1\\).  \n   Consider the point \\((1,m)\\in\\Delta_{m}\\).  \n   The line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\; y-1=-(m-1)(x-2) \\qquad\\text{(through }(2,1)\\text{ and }(1,m)\\text{)}\n   \\]\n\n   has slope \\(-(m-1)\\), which is never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus \\(L\\) is\n   sunny.  \\(L\\) contains the whole \u201cfirst column\u2019\u2019 \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m\\}\\)\n   of \\(\\Delta_{m}\\) and also the point \\((2,1)\\).\n\n   Remove from \\(\\Delta_{m}\\) all points lying on \\(L\\); the remaining set is\n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta_{m-1}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Translating by \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b)\\) identifies \\(\\Delta_{m-1}'\\) with\n   \\(\\Delta_{m-1}\\).  By the induction hypothesis we can cover \\(\\Delta_{m-1}\\)\n   with \\(m-1\\) sunny lines; after undoing the translation we obtain\n   \\(m-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_{m-1}'\\).  Together with \\(L\\) we have\n   \\(m\\) sunny lines covering the whole \\(\\Delta_{m}\\).\n\n   Distinctness of the lines is clear: the new line \\(L\\) contains the point\n   \\((2,1)\\) while none of the inductively obtained lines does, because all\n   those lines avoid the column \\(x=1\\).\n\n   This completes the induction and proves the lemma.\n\n3. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   For a given \\(k\\) we take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\)\n   (non\u2011sunny) and, by the Sunny\u2011Triangle Lemma, the \\(k\\) sunny lines that\n   cover \\(\\Delta_{k}\\).  After translating back, these \\(k\\) sunny lines cover\n   exactly the set \\(T_{k}\\).  Hence all points \\((a,b)\\) with\n   \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) are covered, the total number of lines is \\(n\\), and exactly\n   \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n   Since the construction works for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), all these\n   values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0},\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a triangular set\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\nTake the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_{i}:\\;x=i\\qquad (i=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(S_{n}\\) with first coordinate \\(a\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the\n\\(V_{i}\\).  The points that are not yet covered are\n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the translation  \n\n\\[\n\\phi:(a,b)\\longmapsto (a',b')=(a-(n-k),b).\n\\]\n\n\\(\\phi\\) maps \\(T_{k}\\) bijectively onto  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nConsequently, it suffices to prove the following lemma.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Sunny\u2011Triangle Lemma  \n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose\n> union contains all points of \\(\\Delta_{m}\\).\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(m\\).*\n\n**Base \\(m=1\\).**  \n\\(\\Delta_{1}=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) has slope\n\\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); it is sunny and covers the only point.\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume the lemma true for \\(m-1\\) (\\(m\\ge 2\\)).\nConsider the point \\((1,m)\\in\\Delta_{m}\\) and the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\; y-1=-(m-1)(x-2) \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nwhich passes through \\((2,1)\\) and \\((1,m)\\).  \nIts slope is \\(-(m-1)\\); therefore \\(L\\) is sunny.\n\nAll points of the first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le m\\}\\) lie on \\(L\\), and\n\\(L\\) also contains \\((2,1)\\).  Remove from \\(\\Delta_{m}\\) the points of \\(L\\);\nthe remainder is\n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{m}'=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le m+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-1,b)\\) sends \\(\\Delta_{m}'\\) bijectively onto\n\\(\\Delta_{m-1}\\).  By the induction hypothesis there exist \\(m-1\\) distinct\nsunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_{m-1}\\); translating them back gives \\(m-1\\)\nsunny lines covering \\(\\Delta_{m}'\\).\n\nThese \\(m-1\\) lines are all different from \\(L\\) because none of them contains\nthe point \\((2,1)\\) (the column \\(x=1\\) is absent after the translation).\nHence we have obtained \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains\n\\(\\Delta_{m}\\).\n\n\u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the original problem\n\nGiven \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\):\n\n* take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-k\\) (non\u2011sunny);\n* apply the Lemma with \\(m=k\\) to obtain \\(k\\) sunny lines covering\n  \\(\\Delta_{k}\\);\n* translate those \\(k\\) lines back by the inverse of \\(\\phi\\); they cover\n  exactly the set \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nThus all points of \\(S_{n}\\) are covered, the total number of lines is\n\\(n\\), and precisely \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\nSince the construction works for every \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), the set\nof admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
